// Title: ConvertTo-SecureString Cmdlet Usage Via CommandLine
// Sigma ID: 74403157-20f5-415d-89a7-c505779585cf
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Teymur Kheirkhabarov (idea), Vasiliy Burov (rule), oscd.community, Tim Shelton
// Date: 2020-10-11 | Modified: 2023-02-01
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1027, execution, T1059.001
// Description: Detects usage of the "ConvertTo-SecureString" cmdlet via the commandline. Which is fairly
//   uncommon and could indicate potential suspicious activity
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use to pass password to different powershell commands
// References:
//   - https://speakerdeck.com/heirhabarov/hunting-for-powershell-abuse?slide=65
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.security/convertto-securestring?view=powershell-7.3#examples
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ccbb0369507f796db0b0ae6854c8a2be471b9d84/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_powershell_cmdline_convertto_securestring.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ((FolderPath endswith "\\powershell.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\pwsh.exe") or (ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName in~ ("PowerShell.EXE", "pwsh.dll"))) and ProcessCommandLine contains "ConvertTo-SecureString"
